#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -I
"""agent-mail: a file mailbox between agent-deck sessions.

Mailbox: a directory, by default ~/Mailbox ($AGENT_MAIL_DIR or --dir overrides it).
Sessions on several hosts can share one mailbox when they share the directory: an NFS
home, a Syncthing folder, or one machine. Claims record their host (an 8-hex-digit tag),
so a reader checks a claim's PID only on the host that made it; a claim made on another
host is delivered again after 10 minutes. On NFS a new file can take up to a minute to
appear on another host; Syncthing's own temporary files are hidden names, so readers
ignore them. Each mailbox name still has exactly one reader, on one host.

Name: a session's mailbox name is its agent-deck title in lower case: lower-case letters,
digits, "." and "-", starting with a letter or digit, with no "_" and no "--", at most 64
characters and not ending in "-". These are reserved and name no session, sender or
channel: all, everyone, john, johnw, jwiegley, user, human, operator, owner, admin, root,
system, claude, anthropic, codex, agent-deck, agent-mail, and any name beginning with
"john-". Always pass the name explicitly; inherited environment variables can name another
session. Each mailbox name should have exactly one reader.

Message: a regular file named TO_UTC_from-FROM_UNIQUE.txt, for example
nix_20261030T050102.123456Z_from-pi_3f9a1c2e.txt, owned by you and mode 0600. It begins
with From, To, UTC and Subject header lines, then a blank line, then the body. A sender
writes it to a hidden file in the mailbox and renames it into place.

Ownership: a file is NAME's mail only when its whole name is in the standard form with NAME
before the first "_". So "pi" never takes "clio-pi", "pi-2", "pi.v2" or "pipeline" mail.
Older forms (pi.md, pi--..., pi-<stamp>...) and upper-case names are not mail: readers
leave them in place. Names starting with "." are never mail.

Receiving:
- A file is read once its inode has gone 2 seconds unchanged (ctime, which a sender cannot
  backdate), so a message still being written waits.
- It is claimed by renaming it to .NAME-claimed-PID-HOSTTAG-FILE, then printed between frame
  lines that carry a random token for that delivery, so a body cannot fake a frame. Afterwards it is
  moved to .NAME-read/FILE and kept for 7 days, so a reader whose output was cut can
  reopen it. A claim whose reader on this host has died, or that is 10 minutes old, is
  delivered again.
- One read prints a list of its messages first, then the messages, oldest first, up to
  about 12 KB (--max-bytes). A bigger message is shown in part, with the path of its
  copy. Mail not delivered stays waiting, and read says so.
- A file that changes while being read is put back for later.
- Symlinks, FIFOs, hard-linked files, other users' files and files over 1 MiB (or that
  grow past it while being read) are not read: they become .NAME-rejected-FILE and are
  reported once. A directory is never renamed: it is reported once and left in place.
- Printed text has ANSI sequences removed and control and bidi characters replaced, and a
  line that opens or closes a system-reminder, channel or command-output tag has its "<"
  replaced. Names in notices show every other byte than letters, digits and "._+-" as \\xNN.
- A file that cannot be claimed at all (for example, the mailbox is read-only to this
  reader) is reported once and left where it is; read exits with status 2.
- If printing fails, the claimed messages are put back.
- Reads, watch polls and pending runs record that NAME's mail was checked, at most once
  a minute, in .agent-mail-presence/NAME. send warns when the recipient's mail has not been checked
  for an hour, or ever; the message still waits for it.

Never pipe read or watch through head, tail, sed or grep: what they print is consumed.

Commands:
  send --from NAME --to NAME|+CHANNEL|all --subject TEXT [--in-reply-to ID] [--id SESSION_ID] [FILE]
      Send FILE, or standard input when FILE is "-" or omitted: direct mail to a session,
      a post to a channel (at most 8 KiB), or a broadcast to all (at most 4 KiB). Refuses
      private keys and tokens, and caps each sender at 20 posts per channel an hour, one
      broadcast per 15 minutes, and one identical body per target per 10 minutes.
  read NAME [--max-bytes N]
      Deliver NAME's waiting mail once; prints "agent-mail: no mail for NAME" when empty.
  watch NAME [--interval SECONDS] [--forever] [--max-bytes N] [--parent PID]
      Poll (default every 15 s). Exit after the first delivery, so a background task
      wakes its session once per batch; with --forever keep printing batches, for a
      streaming monitor. With --parent (in Claude Code, --parent $PPID), stop without
      reading once that process is gone, so a watcher outliving its session takes no mail.
  pending NAME
      One line for pi-loop's --cmd --on-change. It changes when new mail is ready to
      read, and again as a reminder while that mail stays unread. It does not change
      when mail is read.
  init
      Create the mailbox directory (mode 0700) and its .agent-mail-mailbox-v2 marker. Every
      mailbox other than ~/Mailbox needs the marker, so that a wrong --dir or
      $AGENT_MAIL_DIR cannot claim a project's files; a directory with only the v1 marker,
      .agent-mail-mailbox, is refused until init runs there. ~/Mailbox itself is refused,
      since Syncthing creates it.
  self-test [SECTION ...]
      Run the built-in checks, every section or the named ones, each in a fresh
      temporary home.
"""
